00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.579 align:start position:0%
higher priced medications have been  shown to be associated with stronger  placebo effects now researchers have  shown that higher drug prices are also  associated with a stronger nocebo effect  the placebo effect means you feel  positive effects because you think you  are receiving treatment when you really  are the nocebo effect means you feel  negative side-effects because you think  you're receiving treatment when you  really are it basically this new study  shows that if you think a drug costs  more you might experience stronger  negative side effects to figure this out  the researchers designed two boxes one  blue and one orange they then asked  subjects to rate how expensively thought  a medicinal cream would be if it came in  the box the blue design was rated as  significantly more expensive then  another set of subjects were told the  cream in these boxes was designed to  reduce itchiness and that as a  side-effect the Green would increase  pain sensitivity the researchers told  participants in advance that the Queen  was expensive or not showed them the box  and then had them apply it then they  expose their arms to a slightly painful  heat stimulant people that thought they  had gotten the more expensive cream  reported more pain with the same heat  when compared with those on the cheap  receiving cream the researchers think  this happens because of the perception  that more expensive drugs are more  powerful and would therefore have  stronger side effects fMRI images of the  brains and upper spinal cords of  subjects showed that the increased  nocebo effect was correlated with more  activity in the prefrontal cortex  understanding the top-down and bottom-up  processing of pain in the body may one  day help to treat chronic pain and  understanding how nocebo and placebo  effects are increased and help  researchers design better  [Music]
 

